---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Compressor from "../components/Compressor.astro";
import FAQ from "../components/FAQ.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "What's a typical photo size limit for college application portals?",
    a: "It varies by school and platform, but limits between 50 KB and 200 KB are common for headshot-style uploads (student ID photos, applicant photos). Document uploads on platforms like the Common App tend to allow much more — around 2,000 KB — but those are usually PDFs, not photos. Always check the specific portal's instructions for the exact number.",
  },
  {
    q: "My upload said \"successful\" but I still got a rejection notice — why?",
    a: "A successful upload usually only confirms that your file was the right format and under the size limit. It doesn't check whether the photo itself meets content requirements — forward-facing, no filters, no other people or objects in frame, and clearly recognizable as you. Getting the file size right is a separate step from meeting those content rules.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I use this for SAT, Common App, or Coalition App photo uploads?",
    a: "Yes, for any portal that asks for an image file under a specific KB limit. Just set the target size to whatever the portal specifies. If a platform is asking you to upload a PDF document instead of a photo, this tool won't help — it only compresses images.",
  },
  {
    q: "What file formats do college application portals usually accept?",
    a: "Most accept JPG (.jpg/.jpeg) and PNG, and some accept GIF. JPG is the safest default since it compresses well and is universally supported — use it unless the portal specifically asks for something else.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does the file size limit apply to each photo, or the whole application?",
    a: "Almost always per file, not the whole application — a portal that says \"under 150 KB\" means that individual photo, separate from any transcripts, essays, or other documents you upload elsewhere in the same application.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Resize a Photo for College Applications (Exact KB) | KB Compressor"
  description="Compress your photo to the exact KB size a college application or student ID portal requires. Free, no upload — runs in your browser."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-10 pt-14 text-center sm:pt-20">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white sm:text-4xl">
      Compress a photo for college application uploads
    </h1>
    <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      Application and student ID portals often reject a photo for being too large. Pick the
      size limit your portal lists and this tool will get your photo as close to it as
      possible without over-compressing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-16">
    <Compressor
      defaultTargetKB={150}
      presets={[50, 100, 150, 200]}
      contextNote="Check your specific application portal's photo requirements page — limits vary by school, from as low as 50 KB up to 200 KB."
      ratioWarningKB={null}
    />
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-6 px-4 pb-20 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Why application portals cap photo size in KB</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Most application platforms cap image uploads in KB, not MB, because photos are often
        stored alongside thousands of other applicant records. A modern phone photo can be
        several MB straight out of the camera — far larger than these systems were built to
        handle per file, especially older institutional portals that predate today's high-
        resolution cameras. Getting under the limit needs real compression, not just resizing
        the image smaller on screen, which is why a plain "resize" tool often isn't enough on
        its own.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Compressing vs. resizing — they're not the same thing</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Resizing changes an image's pixel dimensions — making a 3000×3000 photo into a
        600×600 one, for example. Compressing changes how efficiently those pixels are stored
        on disk, largely independent of dimensions. A photo can be exactly the right pixel
        dimensions a portal asks for and still be too many kilobytes, because compression
        quality is a separate setting. This tool handles both: it primarily adjusts
        compression quality to hit your KB target, and only reduces dimensions if quality
        alone can't get there.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">What to check before you upload</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Every college, university, and application platform sets its own limit, so there's no
        single "correct" number — the presets above (50, 100, 150, 200 KB) cover the range
        most portals actually use, but the portal's own instructions page is the source of
        truth. Beyond file size, most portals also expect a forward-facing photo without
        filters, sunglasses, or other people in frame, with your face clearly visible — a
        photo can pass the size check and still get flagged for those reasons, so it's worth
        checking both.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Nothing you upload here leaves your device</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Since application photos are tied to your real identity and often submitted alongside
        sensitive personal information elsewhere in the same form, this tool compresses
        entirely in your browser — there's no server that ever receives the image, so there's
        nothing to worry about beyond the file you download at the end.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQ items={faqItems} />
</Layout>
